{
  "dom_id": "step-q119569",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "What is the length of the side opposite the ${30}^{∘}$ angle in a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle if the side opposite the ${60}^{∘}$ angle has a length of $21?$",
  "worked_solution": "Let's refer to our standard ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14185522/assets/1f7084b210e13a9c2753c3ae8bfcc9302b109cc9584f589c6b8937536ae93682.png)\n\n  \n\nSince this is a ${30}^{∘}$-${60}^{∘}$-${90}^{∘}$ triangle, the length of the longer leg is $\\sqrt{3}$ times that of the shorter leg.\n\nHere, the longer leg has length $21,$ and the shorter leg has length $x,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}21 & =\\sqrt{3}x \\\\ x & =\\frac{21}{\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the radical from the denominator, we can multiply the numerator and denominator by the radical:\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{21}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{21\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}{\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\sqrt{3}}=\\frac{21\\sqrt{3}}{3}=7\\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nSo, the length of the shorter leg is $7\\sqrt{3}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
